Metal-Organic Frameworks with Target-Specific Active Sites Switched by Photoresponsive Motifs: Efficient Adsorbents for Tailorable $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ Capture
The massive consumption of fossil fuels has led to rising levels of atmospheric carbon dioxide $\left(\mathbf{CO}_{2}\right)$ in recent decades. This trend is considered as the major factor contributing to global climate change.[1] Amine scrubbing by using liquid amine solutions is the typical and most widely applied technique for carbon capture and storage.[2] However, this technique is energy intensive because of temperature swings for regeneration, and suffers from the issues associated with equipment corrosion and environmental pollution.[3] In this regard, various porous solid adsorbents have been developed as an alternative to offer an efficient approach for capturing $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ , such as activated carbon,[4] zeolites,[5] and metallic oxides.[6] During adsorption processes using solid adsorbents, $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ molecules diffuse into nanopores and anchor on active sites by physical/chemical interactions, and then are released by temperature/pressure swings.[7] Notably, chemical interactions contribute to selectivity but are difficult to cleave, while physical interactions show poor selectivity but benefit to regeneration. The tradeoff between the two factors presents a great challenge to develop highly efficient adsorbents.[8] In most reported adsorbents, active sites are fixed, and thus it is difficult to achieve both selective adsorption and efficient desorption in one adsorbent under mild conditions.[9] Consequently, the variation of adsorption capacity inevitably requires energy-consuming cyclic adsorption processes. The key to solving the problem lies in intervening adsorbent-adsorbate interactions between active sites and $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ in an effective, energy-saving, and environment-benign approach. This primarily requires enhancing the controllability of active sites during engineering adsorbents.
Recently, developing external-stimuli-responsive (e.g., magnet, pH, redox potential, heat, and light) materials has emerged as a vibrant area of research because of their good controllability with rapid responsiveness and high precision. Particularly, PMOFs, versatile platforms with tunable structures, attract increasing attention because their interactive processes are fast and few undesired by-products are generated.[10] These advantages make PMOFs proper candidates for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption.[11] Zhou’s group synthesized a $Z\mathrm{n}$ -based MOF with a photoresponsive ligand of 2-(phenyldiazenyl)terephthalate.[11a] It is reported that the positional change of the dangling photoresponsive groups can lead to the reversible alteration of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capture. Hill’s group reported a dynamic photo-switching MOF decorated with photoresponsive motifs, and the capture and release of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ is regulated by the structural variation.[11b] It should be stated that currently reported PMOFs for tailorable $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption mainly focus on steric hindrance or structural change.[12] Almost linear adsorption isotherms of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ in such cases indicate poor affinity of active sites for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ . But suitable active sites are extremely important for practical applications, and the preferential binding of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ over other components such as methane $\mathrm{(CH_{4})}$ and nitrogen $(\Nu_{2})$ is significant for the purification of industrial gases. Therefore, relatively strong adsorbent-adsorbate interactions are expected to enhance selectivity. Unfortunately, it remains difficult to shelter and expose strong binding sites merely through steric hindrance or structural change. As a result, intervening chemical adsorption of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ on PMOFs is still an enormous challenge, which requires judicious utilization of specific interactions, more than physical effects, between photoresponsive motifs and active sites.
Herein, we present for the first time the fabrication of MOF-based photoresponsive adsorbents with target-specific chemical active sites (amines) for tailorable $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capture. The adsorbents are constructed by introducing tetraethylenepentamine (TEPA) into azobenzenefunctionalized MOFs (U-azo) (Scheme 1). The introduced TEPA acts as specific active sites for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ with strong interactions. The results show that $\mathrm{CO_{2}/C H_{4}}$ and $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2}$ selectivities of the obtained adsorbents reach 456 and 334 ( $273\mathrm{~K~}$ and 0.1 bar), respectively. Through cyclic irradiation with ultraviolet (UV) and visible (Vis) lights, the cis/trans isomerization of azobenzene groups successfully shelter and expose amines, resulting in a remarkable alteration of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capacities up to $45.6\%$ . Density functional theory (DFT) calculations show that azobenzene groups with different isomers exhibit distinctive interactions with TEPA. When isomerizing from cis to trans conformation, azobenzene groups exhibit little influence on the surface electrostatic potential around the amines of TEPA, and $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ can be adsorbed freely on the exposed active sites. Interestingly, when azobenzene groups restore to cis isomer, the surface electrostatic potential around secondary amine increases obviously, namely these active sites can be effectively sheltered. Such interactions between azobenzene motifs and amines make the strong active sites tunable for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capture upon light irradiation, which has never been reported in current PMOFs to our knowledge.
An UiO-type MOF functionalized with azobenzene groups (U-azo) was first synthesized by the hydrothermal method.[11i] TPEA was introduced into the pores of U-azo by the wet-impregnation strategy with different loading amounts. The obtained materials are denoted as $\mathrm{T}(n)/\mathrm{U}$ -azo ${\it\Delta}n=2.5$ , 5, 7.5, or 10, where $n$ refers to the loading amounts of TEPA). Thermal gravimetric analysis (TGA) shows that the actual loading values are $2.7~\mathrm{\wt}\%$ , $5.6~\mathrm{\wt}\%$ , $7.3~\mathrm{\wt}\%$ , and $10.8~\mathrm{wt}\%$ , respectively. This suggests that TPEA is successfully introduced into Uazo (Table S1 and Figure S1). Infrared spectra (IR) (Figure 1a) show that the representative peaks of TEPA locate between 3500 and 2800 $\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , which correspond to the stretching vibrations of $n(\mathrm{NH})$ and $n(\mathrm{CH})$ .[13] After impregnation, all the peaks can be clearly distinguished in different samples. X-ray diffraction (XRD) patterns (Figure 1b) give the structural information. The appearance of all characteristic peaks of the synthesized U-azo are consistent with that of the simulated and pure UiO-66 patterns, suggesting that the azobenzene groups do not change the crystal growth orientation and its isostructurality to UiO-66 (Figures S2 and S3).[14] For $\mathrm{T}(n)/\mathrm{U}$ -azo, negligible changes can be observed, indicating that the original crystalline structures of MOFs can be well preserved after the introduction of TEPA. Scanning electron microscope (SEM) was employed to record the morphology of different samples (Figures S4-S8). U-azo is regularly octahedral particles, and the shape and size of these particles are well maintained after loading TEPA. To gain an insight into the elemental distribution, the SEM-mapping analysis were also conducted. The mapping results indicate the homogeneous distributions of C, N, O, and $Z\mathrm{r}$ in $\mathrm{T}(n)/\mathrm{U}$ -azo.
The ${\bf N}_{2}$ adsorption-desorption isotherms were tested to evaluate the pore structures. As shown in Figure 1c, all samples display the type-I isotherms, corresponding to microporous structures. The ${\bf N}_{2}$ uptake of U-azo reaches $248\mathrm{cm}^{3}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , and the corresponding surface area and pore volume calculated by the non-local density functional theory (NLDFT) are $682{\ c m}^{2}\ {\mathrm{g}}^{-1}$ and $0.48\ \mathrm{cm^{3}}\ \mathrm{g^{-1}}$ , respectively (Table S1). Pore size distributions show that the pore size of U-azo mainly locates around 8.6 $\mathring\mathrm{A}$ (Figure 1d). When introducing TEPA with the amount increasing from 2.5 to $10\mathrm{wt}\%$ , the surface areas of different samples remarkably decline from 433 to $78\mathrm{cm}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ . In addition, the corresponding pore volumes and pore sizes exhibit downward trends as well. This confirms that TEPA is introduced into the pore channels. For T(10)/U-azo that has the highest loading amount, its surface area is $78\ \mathrm{cm}^{2}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , and the pore volume and pore size are $0.07~\mathrm{cm}^{3}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ and $3.5\mathrm{~\AA~}$ , respectively. This indicates that excessive loading amount can lead to a serious block of pores.
Based on the above analysis, it is apparent that TEPA are successfully introduced into the pores of U-azo, and the morphology and crystalline structures of MOFs are well-preserved.
The adsorption performances of different samples for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ , $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ , and ${\bf N}_{2}$ were systematically tested, and the shelter effects of photoresponsive motifs were also studied in detail. Because there is no specific active site, the U-azo shows poor affinity for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capture and only part of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ molecules can be excluded by steric hindrance. The introduction of TEPA effectively enhances the binding affinity of U-azo for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ . As shown by Figures S9-S12, with the increase of the TEPA loading amount, $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ molecules can be captured by U-azo through chemisorption. T(7.5)/U-azo exhibits the optimal performance, and its preferential isotherm indicates that TEPA greatly contributes to the adsorbent-adsorbate interactions. Further increasing loading amount leads to a decrease of adsorption capacity (Figure S12), which can be ascribed to the serious block of pores. Notably, $\mathrm{T}(n)/\mathrm{U}$ -azo shows a dynamic photoswitching adsorption behavior upon trans/cis isomerization. The optimal T(7.5)/U-azo has a capacity of $43.4\mathrm{cm}^{3}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ upon trans isomerization, but the value decreases to $29.8~\mathrm{cm}^{3}$ $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ upon cis isomerization (Figure 3a). This change is equal to $45.6\%$ of its adsorption capacity (Figure 3b). Similar behaviors are also observed by the other samples (Figures S6-S10), indicating that TEPA can be effectively sheltered and exposed by the photoresponsive motifs. Additionally, three other amines, namely triethylenetetramine (TETA) and pentaethylenehexamine (PEHA) with different chain lengths as well as undecamethylenediamine (UEDA) with only primary amine, were incorporated into U-azo to further study the influence of chain length and amine type on $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption.[17] As shown in Figures S13-S15, the adsorption behavior of the materials containing TETA, PEHA, and UEDA are similar to that containing TEPA, indicating that the chain length and type of amines have no obvious effect on the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption performance in the present system.
$\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ and ${\bf N}_{2}$ were used to study the selectivity of the different samples (Figures S16-S20). The obtained isotherms show that all samples have relatively low capacities on $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ and ${\bf N}_{2}$ , suggesting that TEPA preferably binds with $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ molecules. In addition, negligible changes of $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ and ${\bf N}_{2}$ adsorption can be observed upon the trans/cis isomerization of photoresponsive motifs. This result also confirms that steric hindrance is not the dominant factor that causes the capacity swings of $\mathrm{T}(n)/\mathrm{U}$ -azo. The ideal adsorption solution theory (IAST) model was used to calculate the $\mathrm{CO_{2}/C H_{4}}$ and $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2}$ selectivities (Figures S21 and S22).[18] The results show that the selectivity of $\mathrm{CO_{2}/C H_{4}}$ increases from 12 to 1217 $273\mathrm{~K~}$ and 0.1 bar) after introducing TEPA, and the value is related to the introducing amount. The selectivity of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2}$ follows a similar trend, suggesting that TEPA is desirable active sites for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capture. Taking the optimal $\mathrm{T}(7.5)/\mathrm{U}$ -azo as the example, the $\mathrm{CO_{2}/C H_{4}}$ selectivity is 456 $273\mathrm{~K~}$ and 0.1 bar) upon trans isomerization, which decreases to 92 upon cis isomerization (Figure 3c). Analogously, the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2}$ selectivity is 334 ( $273\mathrm{~K~}$ and 0.1 bar) upon trans isomerization and decreases to 137 upon cis isomerization. The results suggest the preferential adsorption of T(n)/U-azo for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ over $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ and ${\bf N}_{2}$ . Breakthrough experiments were conducted to further assess the selective adsorption of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ over $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ and $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ under dynamic flow conditions (Figure $\mathbf{\nabla}\cdot\mathbf{S}23\mathbf{\dot{\xi}}$ ). For T(7.5)/U-azo, the breakthrough time of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ is obviously longer than that of $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ and $\mathbf{N}_{2}$ , suggesting that $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ has stronger adsorption affinity than $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ and $\mathbf{N}_{2}$ . In order to evaluate the binding intensity of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ , the isosteric heat of the samples were calculated based on the isotherms obtained upon the trans/cis isomerization of photoresponsive motifs (Figure S24). For the optimal T(7.5)/U-azo, the isosteric heat decreases from 69.8 to $56.6~\mathrm{kJ~mol^{-1}}$ during the isomerization. As isosteric heat is related to the interactive strength, this change powerfully confirms the distinctive interactions between TEPA and the photoresponsive motifs. In addition, similar $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption behaviors can be obtained from different batches of T(7.5)/Uazo (Figure S25). Regeneration of T(7.5)/U-azo was also evaluated as depicted in Figure 3d. The adsorption processes were conducted for three tans/cis isomerization cycles. The adsorption capacity can be fully regenerated, indicating the excellent reversibility of the present adsorbents.
Based on the above results, it is firmly believed to exist tunable interactions between the TPEA and the azobenzene groups. As reported before, amino groups play an important role in capturing $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ . Therefore, the interactions between the amino groups of TPEA and the azobenzene groups under different isomers were calculated by DFT to reveal the possible adsorption mechanism. Figure 4 shows the optimized adsorptive configurations and the surface electrostatic potentials of azobenzene groups and primary/secondary amines. The negative surface potentials around primary amine and secondary amine are $-0.049$ and $-0.041\ \mathrm{eV}$ , respectively, which are considered as the two dominant regions for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption (Figure 4a). Upon trans isomerization, little influence on electrostatic potential is observed around the primary $(-0.042\ \mathrm{eV})$ and secondary $(-0.045\ \mathrm{eV})$ amines (Figure 4b), and thus a high adsorption capacity for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ is obtained. Distinctively, the cisazobenzene has a strong interaction with secondary amine, and the corresponding electrostatic potential increases to $-0.017\ \mathrm{eV}$ . This leads to the substantial inactivation of secondary amine for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption, remarkably decreasing the adsorption capacity (Figure 4c). But for primary amine, there was almost no change of electrostatic potential $(-0.042\ \mathrm{eV})$ upon trans isomerization, indicating that cis-azobenzene cannot effectively affect primary amine. Actually, what obtained from DFT calculation is related to the relative position between azobenzene molecules and amine sites. During calculation, the cis-azobenzene group preferentially interacts with the intermediate amine (i.e. secondary amine in this case), and the interaction between cis-azobenzene and primary amine at the far-end is ignorable. However, in the case of only primary amine such as UEDA, the interaction between cis-azobenzene and primary amine becomes unneglectable. As a result, different adsorption behavior is also observed for UEDA-modified U-azo upon cis/trans isomerization of azobenzene. The adsorption results and DFT calculations show that the rational utilization of the tailored interactions between the amine and the azobenzene groups can lead to a controllable adsorption for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ by photostimulation.
By introducing target-specific active sites into photoresponsive MOFs, we fabricated a smart adsorbent for controllable adsorption of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ based on tailorable adsorbate-adsorbent interactions. DFT calculations demonstrate that the interactions between the active sites and the photoresponsive motifs can be tailored by photostimulation, resulting in the changed surface electrostatic potential around amines, and subsequently tailoring the adsorption toward $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ . Because amino groups are target-specific active sites, high selectivities of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ over $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ and ${\bf N}_{2}$ are obtained, and such selectivity is tailorable through trans/cis isomerization. The tailorable $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capture with high selectivity makes our adsorbents potential in selective capture of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ from various gas mixtures. Our study might pave a way toward the development of PMOFs for practical applications by utilizing the interactions between target-specific active sites and stimuli motifs.
